  than render efficiency, and it keeps the whole mutation story in one function you can read. Focus preservation is a
  known, contained problem (re-focus by task id after render), not an open-ended one. Notably, A still gives reminders a
  clean attachment point: they read the same [38;5;153mtasks[39m array, so we get C's main benefit without its machinery.

  [1mDesign, section 1 — architecture and files[0m

  index.html      markup shell, loads main.js as a module
  src/store.js    task CRUD + localStorage persistence; no DOM
  src/tasks.js    pure logic: id generation, due-date/overdue computation
  src/render.js   state -> DOM; no storage access
  src/main.js     wiring: events in, store calls out, render after
  styles.css
  test/           node:test files for store.js and tasks.js
  package.json    { "type": "module", "scripts": { "test": "node --test" } }, zero deps

  The split that matters: [38;5;153mstore.js[39m and [38;5;153mtasks.js[39m never touch the DOM, which is what makes them unit-testable without a
  browser. [38;5;153mrender.js[39m never touches storage. [38;5;153mmain.js[39m is the only file that knows about both.

  [1mDesign, section 2 — data model and storage[0m

  { [36mschemaVersion[39m: [32m1[39m,
    [36mtasks[39m: [ { id, title, completed, dueAt, createdAt } ] }

  [38;5;153mid[39m is [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m. [38;5;153mdueAt[39m and [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m are ISO 8601 strings — human-readable in devtools, sortable as strings,
  and unambiguous when we add reminders. [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is [38;5;153mnull[39m when unset.

  Two decisions worth naming. [1mThe [38;5;153mschemaVersion[39m wrapper exists from day one[0m — it costs one line now and is the
  difference between "add a field later" and "silently corrupt everyone's saved tasks later." [1mReads are defensive:[0m
  unparseable or missing localStorage yields an empty list rather than a crash, because a thrown exception at load time
  means a blank page with no recovery path for the user.

  Does the architecture and data model look right before I go on to the UI, error handling, testing, and the seam where
  reminders attach?

